\begin_layout Title
Diary – Part Ten–J
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
August 2026 – present
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Linas Vepštas
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Unlike parts one through nine in this series,
 this one is not really about the language–learning effort.
 It is instead a private diary;
 a continuation of Part Ten–I,
 which got over–long.
 It is not curated for human consumption;
 I am making the assumption that no human being will ever actually read this.
 Thus,
 it is filled with random stuff I feel like writing.
 Some of it is very personal,
 some of it is nonsense.
 Mostly,
 I am finding that the act of writing helps otherwise vague and scattered thoughts quantum–collapse into a more coherent form,
 where I can examine them,
 like a dead butterfly pinned down in a display case.
 Dead words.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
If you are interested in this content,
 then you should ask an AI agent to read it,
 then ask the AI agent to pretended that it's me,
 then talk to virtual 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

me
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 I believe that present–day LLM technology is sufficiently advanced to be able to do this.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Part Ten already got an introduction.
 A different way of thinking is about what is going on here is that this is a form of life–logging.
 Or,
 in 18th century terms,
 a diary.
 Just not anywhere near as compelling as those written by the famous diarists.
 This one is more of a mental self–portrait.
 And not even for you,
 but for myself.
 Not to cast a narcissistic gaze at my own words,
 but to organize my own thoughts.
 Still in the experimental stage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
9 August 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There's an earlier 9 August entry in Part Ten–I.
 I split it here because reasons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Off topic.
 I just read a paragraph out of Mary Roach's book:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
At one point he described curing a twenty–two–year–old youth of,
 among other afflictions,
 the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

frequent writing of incoherent,
 rambling dissertations on architecture.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Wow,
 that made me laugh.
 Incoherent,
 rambling dissertations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
—
—

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I'm a mess.
 I think I just discovered that the health of my social network is abysmal.
 And just like someone who woke up to find themselves in terrible physical condition,
 one step away from hospitalization,
 I need to take steps to perform social network repair.
 This will not be easy.
 I even dread it,
 but I have no choice.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10 August 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Slept long,
 slept well,
 feel fine.
 But now I'm going through old lost emails,
 and this sinking sensation appears in my stomach.
 I feel OK,
 but there's like a little birdie whispering in my ear:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Listen to your stomach!
 You should feel melancholic!
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and I kind of even want to,
 to give in,
 to let it settle over me.
 But I'm pushing it away,
 knowing that down that path lies trouble,
 and I've had enough of that,
 thank you very much.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While I dozed into the late morning,
 I again kept thinking about how nothing exists,
 except the here–and–now.
 I continue to find this very mind–blowing;
 its just not how one ordinarily conceives of things,
 because one has memories of the past.
 But those memories are just part of here–and–now,
 of course.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a short time,
 I wondered if here–and–now is a purely subjective experience,
 and that more exists 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

objectively
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 But,
 no,
 that is wrong.
 We have no machines that can travel into the past,
 or future,
 that can perceive the past,
 or future.
 We have no theory for such machines,
 either,
 except for wormholes.
 The here–and–now is a product of physics,
 and not of the mind.
 The subjective experience is apparently in it for the ride.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In short,
 I made no forward progress;
 this was just an echo as I slept.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then I thought of the following diagram,
 from earlier in the diary:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename p10-agents/evolution.eps
	width 40page%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and wondered again how accurate it is to imagine that structure ingresses from some abstract platonic realm,
 into the present here–and–now,
 but then generating pure platonic forms,
 such as the cladogram.
 The point here is that the cladogram 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

does not exist
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 except in the mind of scientists.
 Even species do not exist,
 except as abstract concepts,
 again,
 in the minds of scientists.
 Only specific instances of organisms exist in the here–and–now;
 the fact that some happen to be genetically very nearly identical is an accident of autopoesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is,
 the above diagram depicts an agency that inhabits the platonic realm only.
 None of the above agent 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exists
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in the here–and–now,
 in any literal sense.
 However,
 the side effects that this agent has on the arrangement of atoms in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

actual,
 physical,
 real
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 here–and–now is uncontrovertible:
 Darwin noticed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Of course,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

who is Charles Darwin?
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Well,
 he too seems to be a named agent that also operated in the platonic realm.
 That is,
 his brain,
 having a subjective reality,
 like my own,
 peered into information from his sensory organs,
 constructed a world–model (which is encoded with neurons,
 but 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exists
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in the platonic realm),
 accessed that world model with his subjective–self,
 and spotted a pattern in that world–model.
 Then,
 by hard thought and inspiration,
 he arrived at his theory of evolution,
 which,
 again,
 as a theory,
 exists only in the platonic realm.
 Then he,
 as a subjective individual,
 performed motor actions with his fingers,
 to encode this abstract concept onto ink and paper,
 thus creating a certain complex arrangement of atoms,
 in the here–and–now.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is,
 the human brain,
 perhaps a bit like a ribosome,
 operates in two places at once:
 in the subjective,
 platonic realm,
 and in the physical,
 made–of–atoms realm,
 and leverages it's ability to manipulate physical reality to encode (manifest) platonic ideals as arrangements of physical atoms (in this case,
 ink on paper.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This binding is tight.
 Just as I cannot think without the atoms in my brain,
 so the idea written on a piece of paper cannot exist without the physical paper.
 Insofar as it exists at all,
 it exists in the minds of other humans,
 or,
 in the present case,
 as electronic currents in my laptop.
 And wherever the fuck github stores shit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When I said 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the human brain,
 like a ribosome
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 I was thinking of how a ribosome is made of both RNA and of protein;
 it needs both to bridge the disparate systems.
 The analogy here is that the mind has parts made of atoms,
 and parts made of,
 um,
 subjective reality.
 Is this the case for all agents?
 That they are necessarily bridges between atoms and the platonic realm?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Is evolution a counter–example?
 As drawn in the figure above,
 it is an agent that exists only in the platonic realm.
 And yet,
 it manifests,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

ingresses
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 into the atomic physical world as a collection of biological organisms acting out their lives in the here–and–now.
 Can evolution exist,
 without atoms?
 For the case of Darwinian evolution,
 the answer seems to be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A different way of thinking about evolution is the computer program that implements,
 for example,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

genetic programming
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 A practical example is MOSES from OpenCog.
 It's an algorithm,
 described in a PhD thesis.
 It's actual,
 compilable,
 runnable code,
 with thousands of variants as it was patched,
 bug–fixed and tuned over time.
 Both the thesis,
 and the code,
 are in the platonic noosphere,
 but they do not exist,
 except as certain arrangements of atoms on certain computers that run certain websites.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is the case for mathematics in general:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename p10-agents/mathematician.eps
	width 40page%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The above pictures only one mathematician.
 It is meant to show the mind of the mathematician peering into the abstract realm and discovering mathematical 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

truths
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 But,
 with minor modification,
 it is recognized as a group activity,
 as well:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename p10-agents/math-research.eps
	width 40page%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is kind of like the diagram for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Feminism as Agency
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 All of these diagrams are meant to be analogous,
 although,
 at this stage,
 they are a bit confused.
 What do mathematicians generate?
 New conjectures,
 theorems and proofs?
 Or are these just a collection of historical achievements,
 recorded in written journals existing in the here–and–now?
 What about mathematical discoveries that were forgotten,
 such as those lost in the burning of the Library of Alexandria?
 One can imagine that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

once upon a time,
 they existed
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 which is generally the case for anything that is lost in the past.
 But the loss is very nearly total...
 unless,
 unless...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unless one takes the idea of the no–erasure theorem (or the idea of the unitarity of QM) to it's extreme logical conclusion:
 all information that was lost in the burning of the Library of Alexandria still 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exists
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in the thermal bath.
 Oh wait;
 that's wrong.
 Informational bits can be erased.
 Quantum entanglements cannot be erased.
 So which is it?
 The atoms in the ink written on the scrolls of papyrus or sheepskin...
 was their relative structural arrangements and locations encoded as unitary entanglements?
 Or are there distinct atomic arrangements,
 when evolved into the future,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

collapse
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 onto the same present–day wavefunction?
 How,
 exactly,
 is information 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

erased
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in the physical universe?
 How does one erase a bit?
 Certainly,
 thermodynamic ensembles erase,
 just like bit–shifts erase.
 But ...
 I'm totally confused.
 I do not understand what information is.
 This is a kind of intellectual blocker for me.
 I need to figure out what information is.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Anyway,
 the thought was that if one takes the notion of unitary to a logical extreme,
 then one need only take the wave function of the universe,
 as it is today,
 evolve it backwards,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{iHt}$
\end_inset

 to a point 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 that is before the burning of the Library of Alexandria and ta–dah.
 Not achievable in a practical sense,
 but a seemingly viable thought experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So basically,
 I'm confused.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The problems with ingress and egress are amplified if I consider an algorithm,
 such as MOSES.
 It runs deterministically.
 It is designed to explore a space of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

genetically encoded programs
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 This space 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

does not exist
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 except in the mind of the programmer.
 And yet,
 the running program,
 as it steps through time,
 it explores this space.
 After some amount of run–time,
 it finds some expression that passes the various tests for acceptance.
 This expression is now recorded for posterity:
 it becomes some arrangement of electric currents in transistors in the here–and–now,
 and it stays there until the program exits.
 Before exit,
 the final expressions are printed into a file,
 and,
 in the case of work on the Durkheim Project,
 it gets published in a journal.
 That is,
 a space of genetically–encoded programs,
 which 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

does not exist
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 except in some abstract,
 platonic sense,
 was searched,
 and a portion of which was extracted and placed into the here–and–now,
 and specifically,
 into the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

real world
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of atoms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So what do we have?
 An entirely deterministic program,
 leveraged by a human,
 to extract content from the noosphere,
 to mine content from the noosphere,
 and place it into the real,
 actual,
 physical world.
 This program was called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

evolutionary
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 because it was inspired by Darwinian principles.
 However,
 evolution happened without people;
 whereas computer programs need people to create them,
 and set them running.
 And computer programs exist only in the noosphere (except when their concrete dual is recorded on a disk drive) and human minds fished them out of the noosphere of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

all possible computer programs
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
My head is spinning.
 I can't tell if I'm making progress here.
 Something is somehow ingressing,
 and the fact that a deterministic device,
 the computer program,
 and do it,
 suggests that quantum woo is not required for ingress.
 The only problem with this argument is it fails to address the question:
 where did the program come from?
 As it came from a human,
 it seems that quantum woo might still be required.
 And for Darwinian evolution itself,
 it happens at the biochemical,
 atomic level,
 viz at the quantum chemistry level,
 so there's plenty of quantum woo available for the explanation of why evolution materializes specific DNA sequences out of the abstract space of all possible DNA sequences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is,
 when dealing with abstractions,
 its turtles some of the way down,
 until one hits atoms,
 and then its quantum woo.
 The fact that computer programs can mine the noosphere for knowledge does not evade the need for quantum woo,
 because you have to first explain where the program came from.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I think this is forward progress.
 Maybe,
 barely.
 A scratch in the surface?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I have lots of errands I have not done,
 that I need to do.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
—
—

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
p.s.
 My low–grade upset stomach is almost surely due to undissolved creatine crystals.
 Here I am,
 thinking that I am suffering from heartache and loneliness,
 when in fact its ..
 fucking creatine.
 Well,
 I am suffering from heartache and loneliness,
 but the creatine made it much much worse.
 Fuck.
 We'll give it a few days to heal.
 And resume the build–out of a social network.
 Which remains (very) challenging.
\end_layout

